00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:16.280
In August, 2020, something happened that had
never happened before: the U.S. government

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:19.099
threatened to ban an app.

00:00:19.099 --> 00:00:23.240
President Trump signed an executive order
making it illegal for U.S. companies to conduct

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:28.660
transactions with ByteDance, the Chinese owner
of TikTok, beginning September 20th, unless

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.150
it was sold to an American buyer.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:35.660
Unlike the Huawei ban one year earlier, which
also invoked the “International Emergency

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:41.180
Economic Powers Act”, this was a forced
transfer, and involved something far more

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.640
tangible — something millions of Americans
use every day.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:53.149
And so began, on August 6th, an unprecedented
45 day race to solicit offers, draft agreements,

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:57.149
negotiate terms, seek approval, and finalize
a deal the likes of which have never been

00:00:57.149 --> 00:00:59.050
attempted before.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:01.870
Which companies would the Trump administration
approve?

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.239
How would it affect the presidential election?

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.470
Did ByteDance have any legal recourse?

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.530
How would a ban logistically work?

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:12.070
And what, exactly, would a buyer of TikTok
receive?

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:15.300
The answers to all these questions were fuzzy.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.900
It would seem the app would be split in three:
one version for the Chinese market, as now,

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:26.180
one for select countries like the U.S., Canada,
Australia, and New Zealand, and a third, ‘regular’

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.430
TikTok for the rest of the non-Chinese world.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:33.970
Unsure of whether a deal would be made, TikTok
engineers worked day and night, preparing

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:38.840
to completely disconnect from one of its largest
markets at the last second.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.720
On top of this, ByteDance sued the government
in a last-ditch attempt to reverse, or, at

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.040
least, delay the ban.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.439
Adding to the panic was the confusion around
dates.

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:55.060
Trump continued mentioning September fifteenth
despite already signing an order for the 20th.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:00.040
And on August 14th, the president signed a
second executive order, this time ordering

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.990
ByteDance to destroy all U.S. user data by
November 12th.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.660
American employees of TikTok even wondered
if they would still legally be allowed to

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.970
receive their salaries, before a clarification
was made.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:17.480
Despite all these questions and complications,
it actually did seem as though a deal might

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:18.830
be reached.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:21.230
Mostly because its hands were tied.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:26.420
With nearly zero negotiating leverage and
a fast-ticking clock, ByteDance might as well

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.770
cash out on its inevitable way out of the
country.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:34.680
But, like a well designed rollercoaster, there’ve
been alternating up and down periods where

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.850
a sale seemed either absolutely certain or
utterly impossible.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.580
Sell, but the question was: To who?

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:47.070
The list of possible buyers was relatively
short because...

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:51.270
Even with a Trump-ban fire-sale discount,
it would surely be one of the biggest tech

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:56.530
acquisitions in history — likely more expensive
than Facebook paid for WhatsApp or Microsoft

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.080
for LinkedIn, and

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:03.950
The otherwise likely contenders like Google
and Amazon are already under historic levels

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.930
of antitrust scrutiny.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.820
A deal this high-profile would not help make
their case.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:14.480
At first, it seemed Microsoft and Walmart
— in a joint offer — would take on that

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.590
unenviable challenge.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:21.380
Although Walmart sounds like a bizarre match,
there are several reasons why it might make

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:22.410
sense.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:27.550
In China, influencers, or “Key Opinion Leaders”,
as they’re known there, use the app to sell

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:34.010
everything from books to clothes to makeup
- the modern version of As-Seen-On-TV infomercials.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.140
Imagine Brad Pitt casually live streaming
about a product he likes.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:41.000
A mix of entertainment and advertising.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.540
Chinese “Social Commerce”, as a whole,
is a $168 billion a year industry, and if

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:50.880
Walmart could replicate this phenomenon in
the American market, it might be an effective

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.450
way of competing with Amazon while also positioning
its brand as younger and more relatable.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:01.910
Together, the two companies made a strong
pair — Microsoft could credibly handle the

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.990
technology, while Walmart provided the business
model.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:09.370
The former even made a nod to Trump’s request
that the U.S. government benefit financially

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:13.490
from the deal by vaguely committing to economic
contributions.

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:18.450
The deal was said to be valued at around $20-30
billion and appeared imminent.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:24.340
TikTok’s newly-hired American CEO even resigned
around that time, which analysts interpreted

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:27.710
as a sign an agreement had been reached without
him.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.090
But things took a different turn.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:34.470
With everyone focused on the specifics of
a potential deal, no one paid much thought

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.730
to whether China would approve.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:41.640
A full transfer of TikTok to a U.S. company
would not only damage its global influence

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.960
but, also, and more importantly, set a precedent
that Chinese companies could simply be forced

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.150
to sell on command.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:54.460
And so, for the first and only time since
2008, China’s Ministry of Commerce, and

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:59.250
Science and Technology updated their lists
of restricted technology exports.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:03.730
The new items included quote “personalised
information recommendation service technology

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:08.870
based on data analysis”, and “artificial
intelligence interactive interfaces” — both

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.110
plenty vague enough to be applied selectively.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:16.190
What this means is that ByteDance would need
to request approval for the sale from provincial

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:19.660
authorities up to 45 days in advance.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:25.100
If it didn’t face an impossible task before
this change, it certainly did then.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:30.820
It would now have to satisfy a buyer, the
Trump Administration, and China, simultaneously,

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:32.980
in order to secure a deal.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:38.990
To highlight just how Sisyphean this task
was, its CEO, Zhang Yiming was simultaneously

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:43.640
called a traitor in China and a spy in America.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:49.110
Not unsurprisingly, Microsoft announced its
offer had been rejected, and soon after, Oracle

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:52.040
confirmed it had reached a deal with ByteDance.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:57.820
Although it specializes in enterprise, not
consumer technology, its founder, Larry Ellison,

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:00.400
helped fundraise for Trump earlier this year.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.770
The company’s current CEO was also on the
president’s 2016 transition team.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:10.560
The proposal, submitted to the Trump administration
and eventually to authorities in China, would

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.480
have Oracle become the “trusted technology
provider” of a newly formed TikTok global,

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.240
which it and also Walmart, could hold a combined
20% stake in.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:25.650
The new company would be headquartered in
the U.S., have an American board of directors,

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:28.770
and hire thousands of new U.S. employees.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:32.900
The app’s data and source code would be
stored, monitored, and reviewed by Oracle

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.550
in the U.S., while ByteDance would remain
in control of the algorithm.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:42.310
To be absolutely clear, this is a ‘deal’,
not a sale.

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:47.580
By maintaining control of the algorithm and
engineers in the PRC, very few of the original

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.660
national security concerns would be addressed.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:55.360
In essence, Oracle would be paid to ‘certify’
the security of TikTok — making it more

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.230
like a glorified American ‘inspector’
or ‘custodian’ than an owner.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:05.610
The deadline for the original order was extended
by one week, to Sunday, September 27th.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:10.431
But whether or not this proposal is approved,
in some ways, the outcome doesn’t really

00:07:10.431 --> 00:07:14.380
matter — the precedent has already been
set.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:20.050
As tensions between the U.S. and China show
no signs of improving, TikTok may soon consider

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:23.690
itself one of the lucky ones.

